solution_steps:
We can use synthetic division to divide $ax^3 + bx^2 + 1$ by $x^2 - x - 1$.
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc c}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{a x} & (b + a) x & (a + b) \\
\cline{2-4}
x^2 - x - 1 & a x^3 & + b x^2 & + 1 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{- a x^3} & + a x^2 & + a \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & + (b - a) x^2 & + (1 + a) \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & - (b - a) x^2 & + (b - a) x & \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & + (1 + b) x & + (a + b + 1) \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & - (1 + b) x & + (1 + b) & \\
\cline{4-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0 & + (a + b + 2)
\end{array}
\]
Since $x^2 - x - 1$ is a factor of $ax^3 + bx^2 + 1$, the remainder must be zero. This means that $a + b + 2 = 0$, so $b = \boxed{-2}$.
The answer is: -2

