The matrix whose column vectors are $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$, $\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}$, and $\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}$ can be expressed as
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} \\
\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c}
\end{pmatrix}
+ \begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c} \\
\mathbf{a}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]Applying the property of determinants that says "if a matrix has two identical rows or columns, then its determinant is 0," the determinant of this matrix is
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c} \\
\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}
\end{vmatrix}
&= \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix}
+ \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c} \\
\mathbf{a}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix}
+ \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix} \\
&= 2 \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{a} \\
\mathbf{b} \\
\mathbf{c}
\end{vmatrix}.
\end{align*}Therefore, the determinant of the matrix whose column vectors are $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}$, $\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}$, and $\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}$ is $\boxed{2D}$.The answer is: 2D

